<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ryotwari">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ryotwari</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ryotwari system</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ryotwari system was a land revenue system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, associated with Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-munro">Thomas Munro</a>, under which the colonial government collected revenue directly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">ryots</a>, the individual cultivators, rather than through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> described it in 1857, each registered holder was recognised as proprietor, could sublet, sell or mortgage his land, and could not be ejected while he paid a fixed money assessment. Used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> Presidency, where Munro became governor in 1820, it was extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>. Munro cut the demand from a half to a third of the gross produce, and payment in cash left cultivators at the mercy of moneylenders when crops failed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ryotwari.jpg" alt="Ryotwari" width="330" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Archer Shee · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Thomas_Munro,_1st_Bt_by_Sir_Martin_Archer_Shee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ryotwari was one of the three main systems by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> collected revenue from the cultivators of agricultural land, the revenue and rents being taken together. Where the demand fell on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">ryots</a> themselves the assessment was called ryotwari; where it was made through agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> it was called zamindari. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> presidencies, Assam Province and British Burma the zamindar usually had no place as a middleman between the government and the farmer. The system kept many features of the revenue system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>'s account, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">ryot</a> could each year enlarge or reduce his holding or give it up entirely, and remissions were granted when crops were wholly or partly lost in bad seasons. The money assessment did not change from year to year, though an extra rent was charged for water drawn from a government source of irrigation, and nothing was added for improvements made at the ryot's own expense. The annual settlements only fixed how much of the assessment a ryot would pay after changing his cultivation; they did not reassess the land, and a ryot whose holding was unchanged received no fresh patta or lease.</p><p>Payment in cash rather than in kind was introduced in the late 18th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> sought a monopoly as buyer of Indian goods. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, by contrast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> bid for the right to collect, the highest bidder became owner of the land, and the cultivators became tenants who paid him in cash. In northern India Edward Colebrooke and successive Governors-General urged the Company's Court of Directors, in vain, to settle the land tax permanently.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Critics have held that the demand, even at a third of the produce, was excessive, and that a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">ryot</a> who could not pay could lose his land to the government, which made him a lessee of the administration rather than an owner. Cash payment, in this view, forced sales of land, turned it into a commodity and led to fragmentation of holdings, while revenue pressure pushed more land into cultivation and taxation. The system was in operation for nearly 50 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ryotwari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
